      "note": "The root itself is real and correctly labelled: Pokorny's second entry on p.105-106 is '2. bhā- \"sprechen\"' (Gk. φημί, Lat. for/fārī, Arm. ban, ai. bhánati, ahd. bannan), and Watkins/AHD has 'bha-2. To speak. Contracted from *bhaə-. [Pok. 2. bhā- 105]'. Nourai's page reference POK:105 is exact. The second half of his gloss, 'heal', is also Pokorny's, but it rests on two very different pillars. (a) The Slavonic branch is solid and uncontroversial: Pokorny 106 lists 'russ.-serb.-ksl. bajǫ, bajati \"erzählen, besprechen, heilen\", aksl. balьji, Gen. -ьję \"Arzt\"' — healing by spell, the same semantic bridge Buck describes at 4.87 ('reflect the primitive antecedent of the physician, the \"conjurer\" or \"medicine man\"'). (b) The Indo-Iranian branch, which is the only one Nourai actually draws (node #1), is not Pokorny's own view but a bare citation of Kuiper: 'Nach Kuiper (AO. XII 262) hierher (*bhā-s-) ai. bhiṣákti \"heilt\", bhiṣáj- \"Arzt\", jav. -biš- \"heilend\"'. Modern Iranian scholarship has abandoned that: Cheung 2007 s.v. *bisaz (*baišaz) 'to heal, cure' says flatly 'This root has no IE etymology and may reflect an ancient cultural borrowing, on which see Lubotsky, Early Contacts: 310', and his PIE/Pokorny line is left empty. Buck 4.86 already wrote 'Skt. bhiṣaj- …, Av. bišaz-, etym.?'. English Wiktionary's *bʰeh₂- page lists no Iranian or Indic word for 'heal' among the descendants. So: 'to speak' confirmed, 'heal' true only of the Slavonic sub-branch and rejected for the Iranian words that make up Nourai's whole left-hand branch.",
